// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// literature-rules.esl — a rule PINNED FROM THE LITERATURE, not derived from this document.
//
// In English:
//
//   For any model m: if m has WRN's exonuclease activity, then m requires WRN's helicase activity.
//
//   ∀ m. HasActivity(m, WRN, exonuclease)  →  RequiresActivity(m, WRN, helicase)
//
// This is the piece that makes a sentence justifiable by INFERENCE rather than by having been
// written. The document's activity sentence asserts its own content; the same content also FOLLOWS
// from the measurement sentence plus this rule, and that second justification stands whether or not
// the document says it. `inference.esl` is the step that performs the application.
//
// ILLUSTRATIVE, NOT A FINDING. This implication is invented for the demo — it is not a result of
// Chan et al. The demo is about how a sentence becomes justified, not about WRN biology. The
// `rationale` field below says so on the chain, where a reader of the graph will see it.
//
// ── WHY IT QUANTIFIES OVER THE MODEL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The sentences it relates are about MSI cancer models. A rule that dropped the model from its
// arity would hide a universal quantification there: a claim about one experimental context would
// silently become a claim about WRN as such, never stated and never justified.
//
// With the model an explicit parameter, the quantifier is where it belongs — in the rule, visible,
// and it is what the rule actually says. `inference.esl` eliminates it with
// `JustifiedBy.spec_poly` at the model the sentences are about, which is the ordinary way to use a
// general claim on a particular case.
//
// ── AND WHY IT IS THE ONLY DECLARED THING HERE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// This rule is Declared because it IS an assertion: somebody read a paper and asserts the
// dependency. That is the honest grade for it.
//
// The LIFT from prose to domain vocabulary Declares nothing: `onco-typed.esl` defines
// `HasActivity` and `RequiresActivity` as transparent definitions over the parser's own lexicon,
// so `HasActivity(m, g, a)` and the parsed sentence are the same term — the kernel computes that,
// nothing asserts it. One `DeclarationTrace` sits on this branch, below, and it is the only claim
// anyone is standing behind. (Its antecedent IS a parse — `exists` and `eigentt:fst` are ESL
// surface — reached through a definition rather than spelled out.)
//
// Loaded onto `main` before the two demo branches are cut, so both branches share it.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

namespace core       = "urn:eigenius:core";
namespace reflection = "urn:eigenius:reflection";
namespace onco2      = "urn:eigenius:demo:onco-typed";
namespace umlscui    = "urn:eigenius:umlscui";
namespace wn         = "urn:eigenius:wn";

// The classes the rule mentions, drawn from two different lexicons the graph already held:
//
//   umlscui:C0388246   WRN           (a UMLS concept)
//   wn:n14606137       exonuclease   (a WordNet synset)
//   umlscui:C0920283   helicase      (a UMLS concept)
//
// The model is not fixed here — that is the point of the quantifier.

// ── The rule ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `reflection:DeclaredResource` is the epistemic class for something a human asserts as an axiom,
// definition, or design decision — as opposed to something observed, derived, or machine-verified.
// The class requires `declared_by` and recommends `rationale` and `timestamp`. Nothing about a
// Declared resource claims it is true; it claims someone stood behind it, and says who.
resource onco2:lit_rule_exo_implies_helicase : reflection:DeclaredResource {
    // WHO stands behind the rule. A citation slot: in a real chain this would identify the paper.
    reflection:declared_by = "literature:illustrative-dependency-rule";

    // WHY, recorded on chain rather than in a comment a reader of the graph would never see.
    reflection:rationale   = "ILLUSTRATIVE, not a finding of Chan et al.: a model carrying WRN's exonuclease activity is taken to require its helicase activity. Quantified over the model, so applying it to a particular one is an explicit specialization rather than a silent generalization. Pinned here to show a published rule being applied to a measured claim; the demo is about how a sentence becomes justified, not about WRN biology.";

    // WHAT the rule says, as a machine-checkable proposition rather than prose. This is the field
    // that turns a citation into something the kernel can apply.
    //
    // `canonical_proposition` is optional in general — when absent, a resource's witness defaults
    // to the bare term `Asserts(iri)`, which records THAT something was declared without exposing
    // its content. Spelling out the implication here is what makes the rule usable as the `A -> B`
    // premise of an `app` step.
    //
    // It must match, byte for byte after D47 encoding, the implication `inference.esl`'s
    // `reasoning:declared(...)` sub-proof writes out — the witness key hashes the proposition, so a
    // rule that says something even slightly different is a different rule. Note that the two
    // `onco2:` calls below are DEFINITIONS: decode unfolds them to the parsed sentences on both
    // sides, so the match is on the unfolded terms and holds by construction.
    reflection:canonical_proposition = type_expr(
        forall (m : Set) =>
            onco2:HasActivity(m, umlscui:C0388246, wn:n14606137)
              -> onco2:RequiresActivity(m, umlscui:C0388246, umlscui:C0920283)
    );
}

// ── The trace ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Why a SECOND resource, when the rule above already says who declared it and why:
//
// The kernel's witness index is built by walking TRACE-class resources, not the resources they
// point at. A `DeclarationTrace` is what emits `IsDeclaredAs(target_iri, P)` — the trace supplies
// the target via `reflection:resource`, and P is read off that target's `canonical_proposition`.
// The rule resource on its own mints nothing.
//
// That witness is exactly what `inference.esl` consumes. Its certificate contains
// `reasoning:declared("…:lit_rule_exo_implies_helicase", ∀m. A -> B, ())`, and the `()` is a slot
// the kernel fills by looking up `(Declared, this IRI, hash of the proposition)` in the index.
// `IsDeclaredAs` has zero constructors, so no author can supply an inhabitant — the lookup IS the
// type-check. Delete this trace and the intact branch's inference stops committing.
//
// This is the ONLY DeclarationTrace on the branch. Counting these is how to count what the chain
// takes on authority: `reflection:DeclaredResource` is a provenance label the ESL compiler stamps
// on every declaration it emits, definitions included, and mints no witness.
resource onco2:lit_rule_exo_implies_helicase_trace : reflection:DeclarationTrace {
    reflection:resource    = onco2:lit_rule_exo_implies_helicase;
    reflection:declared_by = "literature:illustrative-dependency-rule";
    reflection:timestamp   = "2026-08-03T00:00:00Z";
}
